# Synthetic AI Evaluation, Independent Review and Revalidation Standard

This document is fictional test evidence. It is deliberately complete enough to exercise the
M3.6 evidence, admission, assessment, challenge, decision, sealing and replay controls. It is
not an MAS publication and does not establish compliance by any organisation.

## 1. Evaluation planning before testing

Before testing begins, the owner documents the AI objectives, intended use, evaluation measures,
acceptable performance thresholds, error tolerances and prohibited outcomes. The approval record
must precede the first governed test execution. Any threshold changed after results are observed
is treated as an exception and independently challenged.

## 2. Representative evaluation and retained results

Evaluation is specific to the AI use case, system and model version. It uses representative data
and plausible conditions of use. Results, datasets, configuration, prompts, retrieval snapshots,
code versions and exceptions are retained under a unique evaluation ID.

Testing covers performance, errors, failure conditions and relevant edge cases. Where risks are
applicable, the plan includes robustness, security, adversarial and prompt-injection testing.
Sub-population, fairness and stakeholder-impact analysis is required where people or groups may
be affected by the outcome.

## 3. Pre-deployment review and proportionality

Every release receives a pre-deployment review by parties not involved in development. The review
scope and degree of independence are proportionate to the recorded risk materiality. High-risk AI
requires formal independent validation by competent and objective personnel outside development
and deployment teams.

Formal validation provides effective challenge and covers conceptual soundness, suitability and
quality of data inputs, implementation integrity, evaluation measures, performance thresholds,
testing approaches and results, explainability, fairness and limitations. Developers must respond
to each challenge; unresolved strong challenges are escalated to the Model Risk Committee.

## 4. Findings, residual risk and approval

Findings, limitations, conditions for use and closure dates are recorded and reported to the
relevant approval body before deployment. The validation outcome records residual risk and the
accountable owner. Approval cannot be inferred from the absence of findings.

## 5. Revalidation triggers

Revalidation triggers are defined according to risk materiality. Revalidation is mandatory when:

- a high-risk model, major model version, intended use or decision threshold changes;
- feature composition, training population, base model, retrieval corpus or guardrails change
  materially;
- monitoring breaches an approved performance, fairness, safety or stability threshold;
- a material incident, regulatory change, new failure mode or unresolved strong challenge occurs;
- a vendor deprecates or silently substitutes a model or service component;
- the scheduled interval expires: annually for high risk, every two years for medium risk, and
  every three years for low risk unless an earlier trigger applies.

The owner records the trigger assessment, decision, approver, affected version and due date. A
decision that revalidation is not required must state its policy basis and is reviewable by the
independent validation function.

